# reaction-gate-runs.log — #215 pre-registration audit trail (rule R4/D).
# Appended by protocol: trigger checks, run stages, artifact hashes, deviations.
# Hashes + counts only — never corpus content.

── D1 (2026-08-02) — read-path environment precondition added to the harness ──
CLASS: mechanics repair. Changes NO threshold, margin, floor, cap, seed, window, or
decision mapping. Computes no quantity differently. Logged BEFORE any in-frame
quantity has been computed (phase 2 has not run; the trigger has not fired).

WHAT CHANGED: reaction-gate-eval.mjs gains verifyReadPathEnv(), asserted in the CLI
immediately after H1 and fail-closed on the same exit(2) path. It refuses to run when
UM_TEMPORAL_QUERY === 'true' in the harness process environment.

WHY THE FROZEN TEXT IS BROKEN (not merely inconvenient): H3(b) requires stratified
read-path probes that call doSearch with the fact's own text and demands rank <= 3 with
at most 1 miss per stratum. That hypothesis is stated in terms of doSearch's ranking
behavior as it existed at freeze (f49e62f), when no read-path window feature existed.
The temporal query-resolution v1 arc introduces UM_TEMPORAL_QUERY, which when 'true'
widens the engine fetch and re-ranks results. Work-context fact text routinely contains
"last week", "in March", "since <date>", "today" — so a non-trivial share of H3 probes
would parse to a window and be re-ranked. The probes read the flag from the HARNESS
process, not the server container, so the deployment-side discipline ("never enable it
on the Pi") does not cover them: an operator running the harness with the flag set in a
local .env (the documented `node --env-file=.env` pattern) would silently perturb H3.
The frozen rule cannot detect this — it has no hypothesis over the harness's own
environment, because the variable did not exist when it was written. That is a genuine
post-freeze gap in the frozen text, not an inconvenience: without the guard the failure
mode is SILENT and would corrupt an un-repeatable measurement.

WHY A GUARD RATHER THAN A RULE CHANGE: the accept rule is untouched and must remain so.
This adds a refusal, not an adjustment — it prevents computing under a condition the
rule implicitly assumed, and can only ever cause the harness to decline to run. It
cannot move any quantity in either direction, so it cannot make a gate easier or harder.

MIRRORED IN SPEC: docs/plans/2026-07-31-reaction-gate-measurement-spec.md (deviations).
ORIGIN: temporal v1 spec section 5 C1; surfaced by the paired-Opus review of that arc.

── D2 (2026-08-05) — read-path environment precondition extended to UM_TEMPORAL_DECAY ──
CLASS: mechanics repair. Changes NO threshold, margin, floor, cap, seed, window, or
decision mapping. Computes no quantity differently. Logged BEFORE any in-frame
quantity has been computed (phase 2 has not run; the trigger has not fired).

WHAT CHANGED: READ_PATH_ENV_GUARDS in reaction-gate-eval.mjs gains 'UM_TEMPORAL_DECAY'
alongside the 'UM_TEMPORAL_QUERY' entry added by D1. verifyReadPathEnv() therefore also
refuses to run when UM_TEMPORAL_DECAY === 'true' in the harness process environment. The
refusal message additionally names the R2 mandatory-check window, so an operator who
hits the refusal understands the deadline at stake rather than simply deferring.

WHY THE FROZEN TEXT IS BROKEN (not merely inconvenient): this is the SAME post-freeze gap
D1 identified, on a second variable D1 did not cover. H3(b) demands stratified read-path
probes that call doSearch with the fact's own text and surface at rank <= 3. That
hypothesis is stated in terms of doSearch's ranking as it existed at freeze (f49e62f).
UM_TEMPORAL_DECAY, when 'true', re-ranks doSearch by multiplying every result's score by
an age-derived factor — so probe ranks can move. D1's own reasoning applies verbatim:
the probes read the flag from THIS process, not the server container, so deployment-side
discipline ("never enable it on the Pi") does not cover them. D1 closed the window flag
and left the decay flag open, and the frozen rule cannot detect either, because neither
variable existed when it was written.

WHY NOW: the undated-decay-asymmetry arc introduces the FIRST documented workflow in this
repo that enables UM_TEMPORAL_DECAY locally (a keyed memory-quality-eval run measuring the
undated cohort's recall). Before that arc, nothing turned decay on outside a unit test, so
the gap was latent. It is no longer latent, and the failure mode is SILENT: a contaminated
harness run would corrupt an un-repeatable measurement with no signal that it had.

WHY A GUARD RATHER THAN A RULE CHANGE: the accept rule is untouched and must remain so.
This adds a refusal, not an adjustment. It cannot move any quantity in either direction,
so it cannot make a gate easier or harder — it can only decline to run.

KNOWN COST (recorded, not mitigated away): a fail-closed refusal is not free. R2 requires a
check inside 2026-11-01..2026-11-15 and missing that window is itself a recorded protocol
violation. If UM_TEMPORAL_DECAY=true were left in a local .env, every subsequent harness
invocation would refuse and the window could close unmet. Mitigations: the eval run passes
decay as an in-process run parameter and never persists it to .env, and the refusal message
names the window explicitly. Note "never true in .env" is the correct condition — install.sh
writes the variable into every generated .env, so "absent" is unachievable.

MIRRORED IN SPEC: docs/plans/2026-07-31-reaction-gate-measurement-spec.md (deviations).
NOTE: that spec lives under docs/, which is gitignored by policy — this runs.log entry is
the tracked, tamper-evident artifact that satisfies the frozen §D protocol; the spec mirror
is local-only. MUST be listed in the phase-2 results doc.
ORIGIN: 2026-08-05 undated-decay-asymmetry spec §5.3 / D-9; surfaced by round 1 of that
arc's paired-Opus review, which found the claimed "standing binding" did not exist.
